<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">battlescreen</ansi>

In the web client, a battle opens as a picture: your company on the left,
the enemy on the right, each standing in its 3x3 formation over a backdrop
that matches the ground you fight on. It shows the same battle as the
Combat tab and the story text, drawn for a quick look. The text clients
see no change.

The battle plays out on its own (<ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>). Nothing you do on this
screen steers it, except the two things you could always do: retreat, and
change the company's focus.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Reading the picture</ansi>

- Row 1, the front, stands nearest the line down the middle; the back rows
  stand further out (<ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>). The three columns are three lanes,
  upper (further) to lower (nearer).
- Each of you shows a health bar, and a letter for your role: F fighter,
  H healer, C caster, G guardian, K controller. You wear a small crown.
- When your company is losing (half of it down, or a quarter of its health
  left), the header says <ansi fg="yellow">company faltering</ansi> and a drop of sweat shows by each
  of you. The first time it happens in a fight, companions of weak loyalty
  may hesitate or flee (<ansi fg="command">help morale</ansi>). A companion who hesitates loses its action, and the
  last-blow line says so. A foe that yields or runs is marked the same way.
- When a Shaman calls a weather (<ansi fg="command">help shaman</ansi>), the header names it, the
  rounds it has left and what it does, such as <ansi fg="yellow">fog (3 rounds: foe ranged
  attacks and spells weaker)</ansi>. The Combat tab's Battle view shows it too.
- Enemy health is shown in five steps, never as numbers: unhurt, scratched,
  wounded, badly wounded, near death. Hover or tap a figure to read its
  name and health, and to see whom it strikes.
- A member who has been promoted (<ansi fg="command">help promotion</ansi>) is drawn as that
  class, a Paladin in white and brass rather than a plain warrior, and
  hovering names the class.
- A small coloured mark over a figure is a status on it. A bright mark
  means it is chanting a spell. A flash and a number show a blow, a heal,
  or a miss as it lands. A fallen figure lies down.
- Under the picture, a line tells the latest blow in words ("Wren hits the
  second wolf (6)"), and the title shows the round. Hovering a figure shows
  its own name and health instead.
- In the dark, or against a foe you can't make out, you see one shadow
  instead of the enemy (<ansi fg="command">help light</ansi>). The shadow stands in a place no foe you
  can see holds.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Allies</ansi>

  When a player allied with you (<ansi fg="command">help party</ansi>) fights the same foes with their own
  company, that company stands behind and above yours in a small formation of
  its own, with a pennant and its leader's name. Up to two are drawn; more
  show as "+N more" at the top. You see their blows, spells, falls and flight,
  and how hurt each looks in the same five steps, never numbers, and never
  their statuses or tactics. Each company keeps its own formation and orders:
  you cannot command theirs, and the buttons act on your company only. On a
  narrow screen (a phone) each allied company is a pennant with a count of
  those still standing.

  Tap an allied formation, or its pennant, to watch that company at full size:
  it takes your company's place and yours shrinks into its place, labelled
  "your band". Tap your small band (or the pennant again) to come back. Watching
  only changes the view. The buttons still act on your company, and the view
  returns by itself when that company leaves the fight. An ally's blow on a foe
  that is not in your fight is not drawn.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Animation</ansi>

  The picture plays each happening as the story text tells it. A fighter
  steps in and strikes, an archer shoots, a caster chants and releases. The
  one struck flinches, or blocks, parries or dodges, with a small sign for
  which. Statuses appear and vanish as their lines are told, the fallen
  collapse, the beaten yield and the routed run, and numbers rise from a
  blow or a heal. A hard hit shakes the picture. A fight that ends shows why
  it ended: the foe is down, the company has fallen, or it withdrew.

  The <ansi fg="yellow">Animation</ansi> menu has three settings. <ansi fg="yellow">full</ansi> shows all of it. <ansi fg="yellow">reduced</ansi> has no
  flashes, shaking or travelling shots, and is the choice already made for
  you if your system asks for reduced motion. <ansi fg="yellow">off</ansi> is the still picture.
  Animation only shows the battle: it never changes or delays it, and it
  keeps pace with your <ansi fg="command">help combatpace</ansi> setting (fast, normal, slow or off), which
  the game sends the screen: if a busy fight leaves it more than a round
  behind, it skips ahead to the present. The story text is
  the full record.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Opening and closing</ansi>

  It opens by itself when a battle starts. <ansi fg="yellow">Minimise</ansi> shrinks it to a badge
  in the corner; the battle goes on. Click the badge, or the Combat tab's
  <ansi fg="yellow">Battle screen</ansi> button, to bring it back.
  Untick <ansi fg="yellow">Open automatically</ansi> to keep it shut until you ask.
  When the fight ends it plays the last blow, shows the outcome for three
  seconds, then closes.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Buttons</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Retreat</ansi> sends <ansi fg="command">retreat</ansi>, and the focus buttons send <ansi fg="command">company tactics focus [rule]</ansi>
  (<ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>), as the Combat tab's do. <ansi fg="yellow">Help</ansi> opens this page.

Screen readers: the picture is decorative. The Combat tab's Battle view
and the story text carry everything, and are unchanged.

On a phone the picture fills the screen and allied companies show as pennants
you tap to watch (<ansi fg="command">help mobile</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>
